# --- part 1: Vedic and classical India
E+=[
X('rigveda-aryan-debate',"The Rigveda and the 'Aryan' debate: who brought Sanskrit to India?",H,'disputed',2,-1500,['textual (oral tradition)','linguistic','genetic'],
 "The Rigveda, a collection of 1,028 hymns in Vedic Sanskrit, is among the oldest texts in any Indo-European language. Most scholars date its composition to about 1500–1200 BCE in the north-west of the Indian subcontinent (the Punjab). It was handed down orally for more than two thousand years with remarkable precision before being written down; manuscripts of the Rigveda are on UNESCO's Memory of the World register. Because Sanskrit is related to Persian, Greek, Latin and the Germanic languages, most linguists conclude that Indo-Aryan speakers entered South Asia from Central Asia in the 2nd millennium BCE, after the Indus civilisation had declined. A 2019 ancient-DNA study (Narasimhan et al., Science) found steppe-related ancestry arriving in South Asia in the early-to-mid 2nd millennium BCE, which most researchers read as support for this migration.",
 [('Indo-Aryan migration','The mainstream view combines linguistics, the Rigveda\'s own geography and ancient DNA: a gradual movement of steppe-derived pastoralists, not a single violent \'invasion\', mixing with existing populations.'),
  ('Indigenous Aryans / Out of India','A minority of scholars, mostly in India, argue that Vedic culture developed within South Asia, perhaps identical with the Indus civilisation, and that Indo-European languages spread outward from India. They point to the Rigveda\'s praise of a mighty Sarasvati river, which some identify with the now-dry Ghaggar-Hakra. Most linguists and geneticists consider this view inconsistent with the evidence.'),
  ('Astronomical dating','Some writers, from B. G. Tilak (The Arctic Home in the Vedas, 1903) onwards, have read astronomical references in the Vedas as pointing to dates of 4000 BCE or much earlier; these interpretations are not accepted by most Indologists (speculative).'),
  ('Politics of the debate','Since the colonial era, when \'Aryan\' was misused in European race theories, the question has been entangled with nationalism and identity, which both sides accuse the other of letting shape their conclusions.')],
 ['wiki:Rigveda','wiki:Indo-Aryan_migrations','doi:10.1126/science.aat7487'],'Rigveda',dateNote='Composition usually dated c. 1500–1200 BCE'),
X('mahabharata-kurukshetra','The Mahabharata and the Kurukshetra war: a remembered battle or an ancient nuclear war?',M,'myth-legend',2,-1000,['textual','archaeological','mythological'],
 "The Mahabharata, at around 100,000 verses one of the longest poems ever composed, tells of the war at Kurukshetra between two branches of the Kuru royal family, the Pandavas and the Kauravas, and contains the Bhagavad Gita. The text grew over many centuries, roughly 400 BCE to 400 CE, and is traditionally attributed to the sage Vyasa. Tradition places the war at the start of the Kali Yuga, 3102 BCE. Historians who accept a historical kernel usually link it to the Kuru kingdom of the early first millennium BCE (Painted Grey Ware culture), perhaps c. 1000 BCE; others regard the war as essentially literary. The epic describes divine weapons such as the brahmastra, which can destroy whole armies.",
 [('Historical kernel','Many historians think a real conflict among Iron Age Kuru-Panchala clans may lie behind the story, greatly magnified by later poets; there is no direct archaeological evidence of the battle itself.'),
  ('Traditional and astronomical dates','Hindu tradition dates the war to 3102 BCE; various writers have used planetary positions described in the epic to propose dates from c. 5500 to 1500 BCE, with little agreement between them.'),
  ('Ancient nuclear war claim','From the 1960s–70s, writers such as Charles Berlitz and David Davenport claimed that the brahmastra descriptions recall nuclear blasts and that skeletons at Mohenjo-daro show signs of a sudden catastrophe or radiation. Archaeologists note that no unusual radioactivity has been reported, that the skeletons came from different periods, and that George Dales argued in 1964 that the \'massacre\' at Mohenjo-daro was a misreading (speculative).'),
  ('Modern echo','Robert Oppenheimer famously recalled a line of the Bhagavad Gita, \'Now I am become Death, the destroyer of worlds\', after the 1945 Trinity test, which has helped link the epic to nuclear imagery in popular culture.')],
 ['wiki:Kurukshetra_War','wiki:Mahabharata','wiki:Brahmastra'],'Kurukshetra_War',dateNote='Tradition: 3102 BCE; historians who accept a kernel: c. 1000 BCE; text composed c. 400 BCE–400 CE'),
X('ashoka-edicts','The edicts of Ashoka: a forgotten emperor read again after 2,000 years',H,'confirmed',2,-260,['inscriptions','textual','archaeological'],
 "Ashoka, emperor of the Maurya dynasty (r. c. 268–232 BCE), ruled most of the Indian subcontinent. After his bloody conquest of Kalinga (c. 261 BCE) he turned to Buddhism and had edicts carved on rocks and polished stone pillars across his empire, mostly in Prakrit in the Brahmi script, but also in Kharosthi, Greek and Aramaic. They speak of remorse for the war, tolerance between religious groups, care for animals and the sending of envoys to Hellenistic kings. Over the following centuries the Brahmi script was forgotten. In 1837 the British scholar James Prinsep deciphered it, but the king called 'Devanampiya Piyadasi' ('Beloved of the gods, of gracious mien') was only firmly identified with the Ashoka of Buddhist chronicles when an edict naming him was found at Maski in 1915.",
 [('A model ruler?','Buddhist texts such as the Ashokavadana portray a cruel king transformed into an ideal Buddhist monarch who built 84,000 stupas. Historians debate how much of his \'dhamma\' policy was personal conviction and how much was a political tool for holding together a vast, diverse empire.'),
  ('Secret society legend','Talbot Mundy\'s 1923 novel The Nine Unknown claimed that Ashoka founded a secret society of nine men to guard dangerous knowledge; the story is fiction, though it is sometimes repeated as fact.'),
  ('Rediscovery','The rediscovery is often cited as an example of how a major ruler can largely vanish from historical memory and be restored by decipherment; Ashoka\'s lion capital is now India\'s national emblem.')],
 ['wiki:Edicts_of_Ashoka','wiki:Ashoka','wiki:James_Prinsep'],'Edicts_of_Ashoka',end=-232),
X('nalanda','Nalanda: the great Buddhist university and the fire that burned for months',H,'confirmed',2,427,['archaeological','textual'],
 "Nalanda, in Bihar, India, was a large Buddhist monastic centre of learning from about the 5th century CE, under the Gupta kings, to around 1200 CE. Pilgrim-scholars such as Xuanzang (7th century) and Yijing described thousands of monks studying Buddhist philosophy, logic, grammar and medicine. Nalanda declined and was sacked around 1200, usually attributed to troops of the Turkic general Bakhtiyar Khalji, and was abandoned. Its ruins were identified in the 19th century and excavated by the Archaeological Survey of India from 1915; the site became a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2016, and a new Nalanda University opened nearby in 2014.",
 [('Destroyed by Khalji','The common account holds that Khalji\'s forces killed the monks and burned the libraries, which were said to have smouldered for months. The nearest contemporary Persian chronicle (Minhaj-i-Siraj) describes the sacking of a fortified monastery that most scholars identify as Odantapuri or Vikramashila, not Nalanda by name.'),
  ('Tibetan accounts','Later Tibetan sources, including Taranatha (17th century), describe further destruction and a fire attributed to hostile non-Buddhist ascetics, and say teaching continued in a reduced way for a time after the raid.'),
  ('Long decline','Some historians stress that Buddhist institutions in India were already declining from loss of royal patronage and competition with Hindu devotional movements, so the raid was the end of a longer process. The story remains politically sensitive in modern India.')],
 ['wiki:Nalanda_mahavihara','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1502/|UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Archaeological Site of Nalanda Mahavihara|heritage'],'Nalanda_mahavihara',end=1200,dateNote='Active c. 427–c. 1200 CE'),
X('samudra-manthan','The churning of the ocean: gods and demons pull the serpent Vasuki for the nectar of immortality',M,'myth-legend',2,-400,['textual (Puranas, epics)','mythological','iconographic'],
 "In a Hindu myth told in the Mahabharata, the Ramayana and the Puranas, the gods (devas) and their rivals the asuras cooperate to churn the cosmic ocean of milk to obtain amrita, the nectar of immortality. They use Mount Mandara as the churning rod and the serpent king Vasuki as the rope, while Vishnu, as the tortoise Kurma, supports the mountain. The churning first releases a deadly poison, halahala, which Shiva drinks, turning his throat blue, and then treasures including the goddess Lakshmi. Vishnu, disguised as the enchantress Mohini, tricks the asuras out of the nectar. The asura Rahu sneaks a sip and is beheaded; his immortal head chases the Sun and Moon, causing eclipses. Drops of nectar falling to earth are linked to the four sites of the Kumbh Mela. A famous 12th-century relief at Angkor Wat depicts the scene.",
 [('Religious meaning','Within Hinduism the story teaches cosmic cooperation and the need to endure poison before reward, and explains rituals and festivals including the Kumbh Mela, one of the largest gatherings of people on Earth.'),
  ('Eclipse explanation','The Rahu episode is a mythic explanation of eclipses; in Indian astronomy Rahu and Ketu became the names of the lunar nodes, the points where eclipses occur.'),
  ('Precession reading','In Hamlet\'s Mill (1969), Giorgio de Santillana and Hertha von Dechend argued that churning and mill myths worldwide encode knowledge of the precession of the equinoxes; most scholars of myth and astronomy consider this unproven (speculative).'),
  ('Elixir pattern','Comparativists note parallels with other drinks of immortality, such as Greek ambrosia, Norse mead and the Vedic soma, debated as shared Indo-European inheritance or independent invention.')],
 ['wiki:Samudra_manthan','wiki:Kumbh_Mela',"wiki:Hamlet's_Mill"],'Samudra_manthan',dateNote='Texts c. 400 BCE–1000 CE; mythic time'),
]
E+=[
X('lothal-dockyard','Lothal: the oldest dockyard in the world, or a water tank?',H,'disputed',3,-2400,['archaeological'],
 "Lothal, in Gujarat, India, was a town of the Harappan (Indus) civilisation, occupied roughly 2400–1900 BCE and excavated by S. R. Rao of the Archaeological Survey of India in 1955–60. It had a planned grid, drains, a bead-making factory and evidence of long-distance trade, including a Persian Gulf-type seal. Its most famous feature is a large brick-lined basin, about 214 by 36 m, connected by channels to an old course of the Sabarmati river.",
 [('Tidal dockyard','Rao interpreted the basin as the world\'s oldest known dockyard, able to take ships at high tide, with a nearby wharf and warehouse; this view is promoted by the Indian government and a national maritime heritage complex is being built at the site.'),
  ('Reservoir or tank','Other archaeologists, beginning with L. S. Leshnik in 1968, argued that the basin was a freshwater reservoir for irrigation and drinking, noting its distance from the sea and the lack of clear evidence of ships entering it.'),
  ('Wider significance','Either way, Lothal shows Harappan expertise in hydraulic engineering and maritime trade with Mesopotamia and the Gulf (Dilmun and Magan), which is attested in cuneiform texts.')],
 ['wiki:Lothal','wiki:Indus_Valley_Civilisation'],'Lothal',end=-1900),
X('bakhshali-zero','The Bakhshali manuscript and the birth of zero',H,'disputed',2,300,['textual (manuscript)','radiocarbon'],
 "The Bakhshali manuscript is an Indian mathematical text written on 70 leaves of birch bark, found by a farmer in 1881 in a field at Bakhshali, near Peshawar (now Pakistan), and held by the Bodleian Library, Oxford, since 1902. It uses a dot as a placeholder zero in a decimal place-value system, the ancestor of the zero used worldwide. In 2017 the Bodleian announced radiocarbon dates showing that different leaves date from 224–383 CE, 680–779 CE and 885–993 CE, and publicised the manuscript as containing the oldest recorded use of the zero symbol. The first known written rules for calculating with zero as a number are in Brahmagupta's Brahmasphutasiddhanta (628 CE).",
 [('Earliest zero','Supporters of the 2017 result argue that the oldest leaves push the dot-zero back by several centuries, before the 876 CE Gwalior inscription that was long cited as the earliest Indian zero.'),
  ('Doubts about the dates','Historians of mathematics such as Kim Plofker and colleagues pointed out that a single coherent text spread over leaves six centuries apart is implausible, and suggested the dating needs re-examination; many date the text itself to around the 8th–12th century.'),
  ('Wider story','Placeholders for zero also existed in Babylonian and Maya mathematics; the Indian contribution is usually seen as treating zero as a number in its own right, transmitted through the Islamic world to Europe.')],
 ['wiki:Bakhshali_manuscript','wiki:Brahmagupta'],'Bakhshali_manuscript',dateNote='Radiocarbon dates of leaves range from 224–383 to 885–993 CE'),
X('soma-drink','Soma: the lost sacred drink of the Vedas',M,'disputed',3,-1500,['textual','botanical','archaeological'],
 "In the Rigveda, soma is both a god and a drink pressed from a plant, offered in rituals and drunk by priests; an entire book (Mandala 9) praises it for bringing inspiration, strength and a sense of immortality. The Iranian Avesta describes the same drink as haoma. By the later Vedic period the original plant seems to have been replaced by substitutes, and its identity is not known.",
 [('Ephedra','Many scholars favour ephedra, a stimulant shrub still called hum or homa in parts of Iran and Afghanistan and used in Zoroastrian haoma rituals; Viktor Sarianidi reported traces of ephedra in vessels at Bactria-Margiana (BMAC) sites, although the identification has been questioned.'),
  ('Fly agaric mushroom','In 1968 the banker and ethnomycologist R. Gordon Wasson argued that soma was the hallucinogenic mushroom Amanita muscaria, used by Siberian shamans; this was popular but is rejected by many Vedic specialists.'),
  ('Other candidates','Proposals include Syrian rue (Peganum harmala), cannabis, opium poppy, honey and various psychoactive plants; some scholars argue the drink may not have been psychoactive at all, its effects being religious rather than chemical.')],
 ['wiki:Soma_(drink)','wiki:Haoma','wiki:R._Gordon_Wasson'],'Soma_(drink)',dateNote='Rigveda c. 1500–1200 BCE'),
X('kerala-red-rain','The red rain of Kerala: alien cells or algae?',H,'debunked',3,2001,['field samples','laboratory analysis'],
 "From 25 July to 23 September 2001, red-coloured rain fell sporadically over Kerala, southern India, staining clothes pink; yellow, green and black rain was also reported. A study commissioned by the Indian government (Centre for Earth Science Studies and Tropical Botanic Garden and Research Institute) concluded that the colour came from spores of a locally abundant lichen-forming green alga of the genus Trentepohlia. Similar 'blood rain' events had been recorded in Kerala before and have occurred elsewhere, often caused by dust or algae.",
 [('Algae','The mainstream explanation is that large quantities of Trentepohlia spores were lifted into the air and washed out by rain, although exactly how so many spores became airborne at once is not fully explained.'),
  ('Extraterrestrial cells','In 2006 physicists Godfrey Louis and Santhosh Kumar published a paper in Astrophysics and Space Science suggesting the particles were cells lacking DNA that might have come from a disintegrating comet or meteor, supporting panspermia; Chandra Wickramasinghe also promoted the idea. Most biologists reject this, and later work reported DNA consistent with algae (speculative).'),
  ('Blood rain tradition','Red rain has been read as an omen since antiquity, from Homer\'s Iliad to medieval chronicles; modern cases are generally traced to Saharan dust or microorganisms.')],
 ['wiki:Red_rain_in_Kerala','doi:10.1007/s10509-005-9025-4','wiki:Blood_rain'],'Red_rain_in_Kerala'),
X('konark-sun-temple','Konark Sun Temple: a stone chariot whose wheels tell the time',H,'confirmed',3,1250,['architectural','textual','oral tradition'],
 "The Sun Temple at Konark, Odisha, India, was built around 1250 CE by King Narasimhadeva I of the Eastern Ganga dynasty. It was designed as a colossal chariot of the sun god Surya, with 24 elaborately carved stone wheels pulled by seven horses. The main tower, perhaps 70 m tall, collapsed by the 19th century; the surviving assembly hall was filled with sand by British engineers in 1903 to stabilise it. European sailors used the temple as a landmark and called it the 'Black Pagoda'. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.",
 [('Sundial wheels','Guides and some scholars describe the wheels as working sundials, with the spokes\' shadows marking the time of day; it is unclear whether this function was intended by the builders or is a later interpretation.'),
  ('Lodestone legend','A popular legend says that a huge magnet at the top held the iron-joined structure together and disturbed ships\' compasses, so sailors removed it, causing the collapse. There is no historical evidence for this; engineers attribute the collapse to foundations, weathering and possibly an unfinished structure.'),
  ('Boy architect legend','Another local legend says 1,200 craftsmen could not set the crowning stone until Dharmapada, the 12-year-old son of the chief architect, succeeded and then leapt into the sea to save the workers from the king\'s anger.')],
 ['wiki:Konark_Sun_Temple','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/246/|UNESCO World Heritage Centre: Sun Temple, Konârak|heritage'],'Konark_Sun_Temple'),
]
# --- part 2: dynastic China
E+=[
X('fu-hao','Fu Hao: a Shang queen, general and priestess, found in an unlooted tomb',H,'confirmed',2,-1200,['archaeological','textual (oracle bones)'],
 "Fu Hao was one of the wives of King Wu Ding of the Shang dynasty (c. 1250–1192 BCE). Oracle-bone inscriptions from the Shang capital at Anyang record her leading military campaigns, including one said to involve 13,000 troops, conducting sacrifices and being the subject of divinations about childbirth and illness. In 1976 the archaeologist Zheng Zhenxiang excavated her tomb at Anyang, one of the very few Shang royal tombs found intact. It held over 1,600 objects, including about 468 bronzes, 755 jades, some 7,000 cowrie shells, bronze weapons and the remains of 16 sacrificed people and six dogs.",
 [('Warrior queen','Many historians present Fu Hao as one of the earliest documented female military commanders, and her tomb weapons support her martial role.'),
  ('One woman or several?','Some scholars note that \'Fu\' may be a title and that oracle-bone references across many years might concern more than one person, so the unified biography could be partly a modern construction.'),
  ('Religious role','Her role in sacrifices and divination suggests royal women could hold ritual authority; she was later worshipped as an ancestor.')],
 ['wiki:Fu_Hao','wiki:Tomb_of_Fu_Hao'],'Fu_Hao',dateNote='c. 1200 BCE (reign of Wu Ding)'),
X('zhang-heng-seismoscope',"Zhang Heng's dragon-and-toad seismoscope (132 CE)",H,'disputed',3,132,['textual','experimental reconstruction'],
 "Zhang Heng (78–139 CE), an Eastern Han astronomer, mathematician, poet and inventor, is credited in the Book of the Later Han with building in 132 CE a bronze vessel that detected distant earthquakes. Eight dragons around its outside each held a bronze ball; when the ground shook, a ball dropped from one dragon's mouth into the mouth of a bronze toad below, supposedly indicating the direction of the quake. The text says it once signalled an earthquake that was not felt in the capital Luoyang, and messengers later arrived reporting a quake in Longxi (Gansu), several hundred kilometres away. The original device is lost and only this short description survives.",
 [('Working pendulum','Most historians accept that some kind of earthquake indicator existed and propose a pendulum mechanism inside; modern Chinese reconstructions (1951 and 2005) have been built and tested.'),
  ('Doubts about direction','Seismologists point out that a simple pendulum could not reliably show the direction of the epicentre, and some suspect the account may idealise the device\'s performance; the reconstructions are informed guesses.'),
  ('Wider context','Whatever its exact performance, it is often cited as the world\'s first seismoscope, more than 1,500 years before comparable European instruments.')],
 ['wiki:Zhang_Heng','wiki:Seismometer'],'Zhang_Heng'),
X('taiping-rebellion',"The Taiping Heavenly Kingdom: Jesus's 'younger brother' and one of history's deadliest wars",H,'confirmed',2,1850,['textual','documentary'],
 "Hong Xiuquan, a Hakka villager from Guangdong, failed the imperial civil-service examinations several times. During a long illness in 1837 he had visions of a heavenly father and an elder brother who charged him with destroying demons. After reading a Christian missionary tract in 1843 he concluded that he was the younger brother of Jesus. His God Worshipping Society grew into the Taiping Heavenly Kingdom, which rose against the Qing dynasty in 1850–51, captured Nanjing in 1853 and held much of southern China until 1864. Taiping ideology banned opium, foot-binding and idol worship and proclaimed shared property and equality of the sexes in principle. The war and the famines and epidemics that went with it are estimated to have killed 20–30 million people or more.",
 [('Religious movement','Many historians stress Hong\'s sincere, if unorthodox, Christian-inspired theology; Western missionaries initially welcomed the movement and then rejected it as heretical.'),
  ('Medical explanations','Some writers have suggested Hong\'s visions reflected a psychological or neurological illness during his breakdown; this cannot be tested and others caution against diagnosing historical figures.'),
  ('Political readings','Chinese Communist historiography long portrayed the Taiping as a peasant revolution and forerunner of socialism, while other historians emphasise Han resistance to the Manchu Qing or the social crisis of overpopulation and economic distress.')],
 ['wiki:Taiping_Rebellion','wiki:Hong_Xiuquan'],'Taiping_Rebellion',end=1864),
X('yellow-emperor','The Yellow Emperor: inventor, sage-king and ancestor of the Chinese',M,'myth-legend',2,-2697,['textual','mythological'],
 "Huangdi, the Yellow Emperor, is a legendary ruler traditionally said to have reigned 2697–2597 BCE (dates calculated much later). The Han-dynasty historian Sima Qian placed him first among the Five Emperors. He is credited with defeating his rival Chiyou at the Battle of Zhuolu, and he and his court are credited with inventing silk-making (his wife Leizu), writing (his scribe Cangjie), the calendar, medicine (the Huangdi Neijing, actually compiled around the Han period) and the south-pointing chariot. Daoists revered him as an immortal who ascended to heaven on a dragon. In the early 20th century he was promoted as the common ancestor of the Chinese nation, and he is still honoured at a mausoleum in Shaanxi.",
 [('Deified culture hero','Historians of the 1920s \'Doubting Antiquity\' school, led by Gu Jiegang, argued that the Yellow Emperor was a deity or later invention whose \'history\' grew richer the later the sources were; this is widely accepted among scholars.'),
  ('Memory of a real chief','Some Chinese scholars and the state-backed tradition hold that the legends preserve memories of real Neolithic leaders of the Yellow River valley; there is no direct evidence for an individual.'),
  ('Sky-ascent readings','His ascent to heaven on a dragon has been read by some ancient-astronaut writers as a memory of flying machines; mythologists see it as a standard motif of Daoist immortality (speculative).')],
 ['wiki:Yellow_Emperor','wiki:Doubting_Antiquity_School'],'Yellow_Emperor',dateNote='Traditional reign 2697–2597 BCE'),
X('xuanzang-journey','Xuanzang: the real pilgrim behind Journey to the West',H,'confirmed',2,629,['textual','archaeological'],
 "The Chinese Buddhist monk Xuanzang (c. 602–664) left the Tang capital Chang'an in 629, against an imperial travel ban, and travelled overland via Central Asia to India, where he studied at Nalanda. He returned in 645 with 657 Buddhist texts, which he spent the rest of his life translating, and dictated an account of the lands he had seen, the Great Tang Records on the Western Regions (646). In the 19th century British archaeologists including Alexander Cunningham used his detailed distances and descriptions to locate Buddhist sites such as Nalanda, Sarnath and Kushinagar. His journey became the basis of the 16th-century novel Journey to the West, in which the monk is escorted by the Monkey King Sun Wukong, a pig and a river spirit.",
 [('Reliable guide','His travel records proved accurate enough to guide excavations, making them an example of an ancient text later confirmed by archaeology.'),
  ('Monkey King origins','Scholars debate whether Sun Wukong was inspired by the Hindu monkey god Hanuman (argued by Hu Shih) or developed from Chinese folk traditions (argued by Lu Xun); both influences may be involved.'),
  ('Fact and fantasy','The novel turned a scholarly pilgrimage into a fantastic quest with 81 trials, and is counted among the Four Great Classical Novels of Chinese literature.')],
 ['wiki:Xuanzang','wiki:Journey_to_the_West','wiki:Great_Tang_Records_on_the_Western_Regions'],'Xuanzang',end=645),
]
E+=[
X('dunhuang-library-cave','The Dunhuang Library Cave: a sealed room of 50,000 manuscripts',H,'confirmed',2,1000,['archaeological','textual'],
 "In 1900 Wang Yuanlu, a Daoist caretaker of the Mogao Caves near Dunhuang on the Silk Road, found a walled-up chamber, now Cave 17, packed with tens of thousands of manuscripts, paintings and printed items from the 4th to early 11th centuries, in Chinese, Tibetan, Sanskrit, Khotanese, Sogdian, Old Uyghur, Hebrew and other languages. Among them is a copy of the Diamond Sutra dated 868 CE, the oldest dated printed book in the world (now in the British Library). In 1907–08 the explorers Aurel Stein and Paul Pelliot acquired large parts of the collection cheaply, and the rest was dispersed to Beijing, St Petersburg, Japan and elsewhere. The International Dunhuang Project now reunites them digitally.",
 [('Hidden from invaders','A common explanation is that the monks sealed the cave in the early 11th century to protect its contents from an expected invasion, by the Tanguts (Xi Xia) or by the Muslim Karakhanids who had conquered Buddhist Khotan in 1006.'),
  ('Sacred waste store','Others, following Fujieda Akira, propose that the cave was a repository for worn-out or obsolete sacred texts that could not be destroyed, which would explain the many fragments and duplicates.'),
  ('Rescue or looting?','Stein and Pelliot are seen by some as having saved the material for scholarship and by others, especially in China, as having taken national treasures; the episode remains part of debates on the return of cultural property.')],
 ['wiki:Library_Cave','wiki:Diamond_Sutra','wiki:Mogao_Caves'],'Library_Cave',dateNote='Sealed c. 1000 CE; rediscovered 1900'),
X('hongshan-jade-dragon','The Hongshan jade dragons: the first Chinese dragon?',H,'confirmed',3,-3500,['archaeological'],
 "The Hongshan culture (c. 4700–2900 BCE) of Inner Mongolia and Liaoning, north-east China, is known for finely worked jades, including curled 'pig-dragons' (zhulong) and a 26 cm C-shaped jade dragon found at Sanxingtala in 1971. At Niuheliang, archaeologists found a 'Goddess Temple' with clay statues and large stone burial mounds. Even earlier, a dragon and a tiger made of mussel shells flank a burial at Xishuipo (Puyang, Henan, c. 4000 BCE). The dragon later became a central symbol of rain, water, emperors and good fortune in Chinese culture.",
 [('Animal origins','Scholars have proposed many models for the Chinese dragon: pigs, snakes, alligators, fish, silkworms or a combination of clan totems; the curled Hongshan jades look to some like embryos or larvae.'),
  ('Fossil inspiration','For centuries Chinese apothecaries sold fossil bones and teeth as \'dragon bones\'; some writers argue that fossils influenced dragon legends, as folklorist Adrienne Mayor has argued for Greek monsters, though this is hard to prove for the earliest Chinese images.'),
  ('Lost-civilisation claims','Some popular writers present Hongshan as a lost early civilisation or the single source of Chinese culture; archaeologists see it as one of several interacting Neolithic cultures that contributed to later Chinese civilisation.')],
 ['wiki:Hongshan_culture','wiki:Chinese_dragon'],'Hongshan_culture',end=-2900,dateNote='Hongshan culture c. 4700–2900 BCE'),
X('great-wall','The Great Wall of China and the myth that it can be seen from space',H,'confirmed',2,-700,['archaeological','textual','remote sensing'],
 "The 'Great Wall' is really many walls built by different Chinese states and dynasties from around the 7th century BCE to the 17th century CE to guard against northern peoples. The first emperor, Qin Shi Huang, linked earlier walls after 221 BCE; most of the brick-and-stone wall visible today was built by the Ming dynasty (1368–1644). A 2012 Chinese survey measured all wall sections at about 21,196 km. It is a UNESCO World Heritage Site.",
 [('Visible from space?','A widely repeated claim, already in print in the 1930s, says the wall is the only human-made structure visible from the Moon or from space. NASA and astronauts, including China\'s first astronaut Yang Liwei in 2003, say it is not visible to the naked eye from the Moon and is very hard to see even from low Earth orbit, because it is narrow and similar in colour to its surroundings.'),
  ('Meng Jiangnu legend','A famous folk tale says that Lady Meng Jiang\'s husband died as a forced labourer on the Qin wall, and her weeping was so bitter that a section of the wall collapsed to reveal his bones; it expresses popular memory of the suffering of wall builders.'),
  ('Bodies in the wall','The common claim that workers\' bodies were buried inside the wall is not supported by archaeological evidence, though many labourers certainly died during construction.')],
 ['wiki:Great_Wall_of_China','url:https://www.nasa.gov/image-article/great-wall/|NASA: Great Wall (image article)|science agency','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/438/|UNESCO World Heritage Centre: The Great Wall|heritage'],'Great_Wall_of_China',end=1644,dateNote='c. 7th century BCE to 1644 CE'),
X('kaifeng-jews','The Jews of Kaifeng: a Jewish community in imperial China',H,'confirmed',3,1000,['inscriptions','textual'],
 "A Jewish community lived in Kaifeng, the capital of the Northern Song dynasty, probably from around the 10th–11th centuries. A stele of 1489 says Jews arrived during the Song, bringing cotton goods from the 'Western regions', and built a synagogue in 1163. In 1605 the Jesuit Matteo Ricci met a Kaifeng Jew, Ai Tian, who had come to Beijing, bringing the community to European attention. Through intermarriage, Chinese education and the loss of rabbis, the community gradually assimilated; the synagogue, repeatedly damaged by Yellow River floods, was not rebuilt after the mid-19th century. A few hundred to around a thousand people in Kaifeng today identify as descendants, and some have emigrated to Israel.",
 [('Silk Road merchants','Most historians think the founders were Persian-speaking Jewish merchants who came via the Silk Road or by sea; the community\'s texts include Persian words.'),
  ('Earlier origins','A stele of 1663 claims an arrival as early as the Han dynasty, and other claims connect them to the Lost Tribes of Israel; these are not supported by firm evidence (speculative).'),
  ('Identity today','Chinese authorities do not recognise the descendants as a separate ethnic group or Judaism as an official religion, and their status remains sensitive.')],
 ['wiki:Kaifeng_Jews'],'Kaifeng_Jews'),
X('hanging-coffins','The hanging coffins of China: how were they placed on sheer cliffs?',H,'disputed',3,-1000,['archaeological','textual'],
 "In southern China, notably in the Wuyi Mountains (Fujian), the Three Gorges and the Bo people's homeland in Gongxian, Sichuan, wooden coffins were placed in caves or on wooden beams driven into sheer cliff faces, sometimes more than 100 m above the ground. The oldest are thought to be around 3,000 years old, and the practice continued in some places into the Ming dynasty. The Bo, who used the custom, largely disappear from records after a Ming military campaign against them in 1573. Similar cliff burials exist at Sagada in the Philippines and among the Toraja of Sulawesi.",
 [('How they were raised','Proposed methods include lowering coffins by ropes from the cliff top, building scaffolding or plank walkways, or floating them up on high water; researchers have demonstrated rope-and-winch methods, but no contemporary description survives.'),
  ('Why so high','Explanations include protecting the dead from animals and robbers, bringing the dead closer to heaven, and showing filial piety; the meaning probably differed between cultures.'),
  ('Vanished people','The fate of the Bo has been the subject of speculation; most scholars think survivors assimilated into neighbouring populations after 1573.')],
 ['wiki:Hanging_coffins','wiki:Bo_people'],'Hanging_coffins',dateNote='Oldest c. 3,000 years old; practice into the Ming dynasty'),
]
